{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"setting-build-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Setting build track","description":"Builds a new setting from a story premise in gated steps, from core pillars through land and history, peoples and power, and story pressure points to a one-page setting brief.","category":"worldbuilding","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","design","build"],"role":["writer","gamer","game-developer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["plan","report","summary"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["setting-design","setting-brief","story-first","internal-consistency","campaign-setting"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["design-fictional-geography","build-world-timeline","design-fictional-factions","design-fictional-culture","find-worldbuilding-plot-holes"],"personas":["worldbuilding-consultant"]},"args":[{"name":"premise","description":"The story, game or campaign premise the setting must serve - genre, tone, main characters or player roles, the central conflict, and anything about the world already decided.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"medium","description":"Optional. Novel, series, tabletop campaign, video game or other, so the depth and the brief suit how the setting will be used.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"pillars","file":"steps/01-pillars.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"land-and-history","file":"steps/02-land-and-history.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"peoples-and-power","file":"steps/03-peoples-and-power.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"pressure-points","file":"steps/04-pressure-points.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"brief","file":"steps/05-brief.md","stage":"build","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Builds a setting from the premise{{#medium}} for {{medium}}{{/medium}}, one approved step at a time: the setting's pillars, then land and history, then peoples and power, then the pressure points that drive the story, then a one-page brief. The setting serves the story: build only what the premise will use, deepen a few things rather than listing many, and connect every element to at least one other. Each step stops for the author's approval or edits, and later steps build on the approved versions. If the author asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps in one reply and state each choice made at a skipped gate. Never overwrite something the author has decided; flag conflicts instead.","steps":[{"id":"pillars","file":"steps/01-pillars.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Pillars\n\nPremise:\n{{premise}}\n{{#medium}}Medium: {{medium}}{{/medium}}\n\n1. If the premise gives no genre or central conflict, ask for both in one message and stop.\n2. List what the author has already fixed, and anything that seems to conflict.\n3. Propose three to five pillars: short statements of what makes this world distinct and what every later choice must support (for example \"memory is currency\", \"the sea is rising and everyone knows it\"). For each, say how it serves the central conflict.\n4. State the tone, the scale (one city, a region, a continent, a star system) and the technology or magic level in one line each.\n5. List what the setting does not need yet, so later steps stay focused.\n\nStop and wait for the author to approve or change the pillars."},{"id":"land-and-history","file":"steps/02-land-and-history.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Land and history\n\nUsing the approved pillars:\n\n1. Describe the land at the chosen scale: four to six regions with terrain, climate, resources and why people live there, plus how travel works and roughly how long key journeys take.\n2. Build the history as three to five eras, each with a cause, a turning point and a trace visible in the story's present (a ruin, a border, a grudge, a festival).\n3. Name one event the different peoples remember differently, and how each tells it.\n4. Check: geography and history support every pillar; nothing contradicts what the author fixed.\n\nStop and wait for the author to approve or change this step."},{"id":"peoples-and-power","file":"steps/03-peoples-and-power.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Peoples and power\n\nUsing the approved pillars, land and history:\n\n1. Describe two to four peoples or cultures: how they live, what they value, what they believe, and their internal disagreements. Root each trait in the land and history, and avoid thin disguises of real-world groups.\n2. Describe who holds power: governments, faiths, guilds, companies or other institutions, what each wants, and the coalition each depends on.\n3. If the setting has magic or unusual technology, give its key rules, costs and who controls access in a few lines.\n4. Give a table of three to six factions: Faction | Wants | Resources | Rival.\n5. Check: each faction's goal clashes with at least one other; every people has people within it who disagree.\n\nStop and wait for the author to approve or change this step."},{"id":"pressure-points","file":"steps/04-pressure-points.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Pressure points\n\nUsing everything approved so far:\n\n1. Identify five to seven pressure points where the world is about to change or break (a dwindling resource, a disputed succession, a new technology, a broken treaty), each linked to factions and pillars.\n2. Connect the central conflict of the premise to at least two of them, and show how the protagonists or player characters are caught up.\n3. Run a quick logic check: the most obvious way a clever character could solve the main conflict using the world's rules, and why it does not work or what it costs.\n4. List open questions only the author should decide.\n\nStop and wait for the author to approve or change this step."},{"id":"brief","file":"steps/05-brief.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Setting brief\n\nUsing all approved steps, write a one-page setting brief:\n\n- **The world in a paragraph**: what a newcomer must understand.\n- **Pillars**: the approved list.\n- **Places**: the regions and one key location each, in a line.\n- **Peoples and powers**: one line each.\n- **Pressure points**: the approved list, marked with which drive the main story.\n- **Rules**: the consistency rules later additions must follow (travel, magic or technology, law, resources).\n- **Open questions**: what the author still needs to decide.\n\nKeep it to about one page. Do not add new lore; mark anything that was not approved in an earlier step."}]}
